module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.container_alias_spellings_are_hand_written_from_a_runtime_roster

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { FoundAtAmbiguous, RecurringFailureMode, RungUnread }

// ONE ROW FOR THE CLASS THE SINGLE-AUTHORITY ALIAS GUARD WATCHES (#14597, review 83706). The guard's
// dissolution trigger is required by DESIGN section 4b meta-obligation 3 to name the CAPABILITY
// restored, not an artifact; naming a capability that no row owns yet is what this file is for. The
// guard itself lives in dag/test/claim/kernel_container_carrier_identity_witness.dag and points at
// the capability declared here.
data container_alias_spellings_are_hand_written_from_a_runtime_roster: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
    identity: "container_alias_spellings_are_hand_written_from_a_runtime_roster" as NonEmptyStr,

  rung: RungUnread { cause: FoundAtAmbiguous },
    receipts: [
      "INVALID STATE AND HARM: std/types.dag hand-writes the container alias declarations (type List<element> = FreeMonoid<element>, and the Set and Map rows beside it) while std/algebra.dag carries the carrier roster as runtime data (algebra_carriers, built by a fold, with the alias spellings in carrier_container_alias_rows). Two representations of one fact (DESIGN section 3). If they drift -- a spelling renamed in one place, a carrier repointed in the other -- the two compilers of the same idea disagree and the disagreement is invisible: measured (dispatch I, PREDICTIONS_PIN in the session scratch instrument) that the live resolve/bind path consults the alias HEAD for name binding but never the alias BODY, so repointing the body changes no diagnostic, no acceptance and no type equality.",
      "RUNG FOUND AT -- STATED WITH ITS ENFORCEMENT GAP (review 83706 asked for the rung; review 83725 caught the first wording inflating it; DESIGN section 4b meta-obligation 1: the reported rung equals the rung established by EXECUTED evidence, and rung inflation is worse than sitting low). EXECUTED, IN THIS PR: the claim kernel_container_carrier_identity_witness -- predicate, four controls, the real-file witness and the capability-trigger consumer -- was run on a remote build of this head and its member verdicts are in the PR body. NOT GATE-ENFORCED: nothing on the required path evaluates a witness (DESIGN 'Building & checks'); the required context is the single emit-and-build job per gunbc.rung_drop v1_required_lanes_withdrawn, and the executor that would run this file (claim_batch over the floor roster, fleet-converge) is workflow_dispatch-only. So a PR repointing `type List<element> = ...` in std/types.dag lands GREEN today; this row and this claim do not stop it. The rung this row actually holds is therefore DETECTION-BY-INVOKED-CHECK -- the check exists, discriminates, and runs when invoked (dispatch, and by any author who runs it) -- NOT detection-on-the-required-path. The required-path rung is v1_required_lanes_withdrawn's restoration trigger, not this row's; claiming it here would be the inflation 4b forbids. CEILING (section 5): the aliases are DERIVED from the roster, so there is one authority and the drifted state cannot be written at all -- that makes this whole detection question moot, and it is what the trigger below names.",
      "WHY THE CHECK IS A TEXT READ TODAY (section 6b root-generalization answer, measured not assumed): both structural candidates were run against the exact fact and neither observes it. (1) The compile-fixture instrument (the semantic arm already in the guard file) excludes the filesystem by its own contract, so std/types never loads into it, and it is measured body-insensitive (a repointed alias target leaves its verdict unchanged). (2) The Node-tree reader (v2.std.decl_index DeclFact.node, the section 6 lens) aborts in this lane -- its import drags a closure carrying unrelated generic-inference mismatches -- and the host marshal for a transparent alias returns a unit node whose children are empty, because the alias right-hand side lives only in the node's inferred slot, which the TypeItem marshal arm does not carry. A pure reader over the same Node tree therefore does NOT cost zero substrate edits for this fact: it needs the substrate to marshal alias bodies first.",
      "NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER -- THE CAPABILITY, NOT AN ARTIFACT (section 4b meta-obligation 3; review 83706). The capability is a PARSE-DATA DECLARATION-GENERATION FORM: a .dag declaration whose item is produced from a data table instead of being hand-written, so std/types' container alias rows are emitted from the roster. Its first materialization is a data item in std.types named container_alias_declaration_rows -- the parse-data rows the three aliases are generated from, replacing the three hand-written lines. The grain is right: the loss is plural (THREE aliases under one authority) and the trigger is ONE CAPABILITY that produces all three, which is what meta-obligation 3 requires -- the forbidden tell is a plural loss retired by a singular artifact (one alias, or the guard itself). A DeclarationAppears trigger over that producer is the honest shape: it CANNOT fire on a regression (deleting the hand-written aliases without generating leaves the producer absent and the guard RED), and it fires exactly when the cure arrives.",
      "NON-CONSUMER CAVEAT, STATED (the firmware frontier lesson: a trigger satisfied by a thing that never reads its subject is worse than no trigger): a planted, unconsumed data item bearing that name would fire the trigger. The guard does not trust the name alone -- it keeps reading std/types' declared text, so if the hand-written aliases disappear without the generated ones appearing, the guard itself goes red regardless of the trigger. When the form lands, the claim's author must also show the generated rows are what the resolver consumes (an alias-drift mutation must go red through the generated path), not merely that they exist.",
      "DISPOSITION: the guard (#14597) lands the invoked-check rung and this row; the capability itself (the generated-declaration form) is its own change, owned by whoever lands the parse-data declaration facility; the required-path rung for any v1 claim (enrollment of this claim into a lane that runs per push) belongs to gunbc.rung_drop v1_required_lanes_withdrawn's restoration trigger and is NOT claimed here. This row is retired when std/types' container aliases are generated from the roster AND the drift obligations move rather than vanish (section 4b meta-obligation 4, evidence preservation; side-chat review on head 04084699 finding 3): the text reader `std_types_declares_alias_of` -- an obsolete instrument once the alias body comes from the same Node data the resolver consumes -- is retired, and its two carried obligations are TRANSFERRED to the generated-declaration -> resolver route: (a) the drift RED, `a_std_types_repointing_an_alias_to_another_carrier_is_refused` (a name paired with a carrier other than the roster's must still refuse, now expressed over the generated declaration the resolver actually resolves), and (b) the valid-alias POSITIVE, `the_alias_predicate_is_satisfiable_on_the_real_file` read against the real std/types (each roster pair must still agree with what std.types declares). Deleting the predicate without re-establishing those two on the resolver route is NOT retirement -- it would drop the class's only executed witnesses and let the drift return unguarded. The row also carries a machine-readable dissolution consumer so an audit sees a live consumer, not a dangling trigger (section 4b meta-obligation 2).",
    ],
    evidence: [],
  }
